{Of all my tournament games, this one sticks most fondly in my memory: a misremembered opening, an obvious win overlooked, a brilliant finish, its total inconsistency is part of the charm. I was 17 and had begun the tournament with five consecutive losses - still a record for Hastings - and drawn in round six. Then came this game.} 1. e4 c5 {Most unusual for Gligoric, who almost invariably met e4 with 1...e5. (Hartston, 2009)} 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 8. Qd2 Qxb2 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. f5 ({At the time, everyone was playing} 10. e5 {here, but I had found an obscure game in which the Latvian player Gipslis had beaten Korchnoi with 10 f5. It seemed worth trying. (Hartston, 2009)}) 10... Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 dxe5 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Be2 O-O ({As Fischer later showed,} 16... h5 {is the right way to play.}) 17. O-O $2 {"I was not quite sure what I was going to do after 17 ... Qxa2, but played this confident that I was still following the Gipslis-Korchnoi game. Afterwards, I discovered that we had already diverged. Creativity often stems from a poor memory." (Hartston, 2009 - he's doubly forgetful because he in fact played 17 0-0 and it was Gipslis who played 17 Rb3)} (17. Rb3 $1 Qa4 18. c4 Kh8 19. O-O Ra7 20. Qh6 f5 $2 21. Rg3 Bb4 $2 22. Nf6 {1-0, Gipslis-Korchnoi, USSR ch 1963}) 17... Qa4 $2 {"Gligoric did not remember the Gipslis v Korchnoi game so did not realise the strength of White's next move." (Hartston, 1966)} (17... Bc5+ 18. Kh1 Qe3 $11 {Clarke}) (17... Bd8 {? Qe7= Clarke}) 18. c4 f5 $2 (18... Ra7 {was the best defensive chance now (Hartston, 1966)}) 19. Rb3 $1 $18 c5 (19... fxe4 20. Rg3+ Kh8 21. Rxf8+ Bxf8 22. Qg5 {wins}) (19... f4 20. g3 {would give an irresistible attack (Hartston, 1966)}) 20. Qh6 ({Everyone in the hall saw} 20. Rg3+ Kh8 (20... Kf7 21. Bh5#) 21. Qc3 {winning at once since e5 cannot be protected. I was so fixated on the diagonal to h6 that I never even considered moving the queen in another direction. (Hartston, 2009)}) 20... Rf7 21. Rg3+ Kh8 22. Bh5 Qe8 23. Rxf5 $3 {I was told that this move was met by comments from attending dignitaries such as those by two leading chess journalists: "What the hell�s he playing at" and "The stupid bastard's messed it up!" It made up for move 20. (Hartston, 2009)} ({Now} 23. Bxf7 Qxf7 {(Stockfish 17 shows White still has} 24. Rxf5 $3 exf5 25. Nd6 $18 {as in the game)}) ({or} 23. Rg7 Rxg7 24. Bxe8 Bb7 {with latent threats against g2 as well as e4 and e8, would be fine for Black. Fortunately, I found something better, which surprised the onlookers as much as my 20th move had. (Hartston, 2009)}) 23... exf5 24. Nd6 Bxd6 25. Bxf7 $1 {Sources: CHESS, January 1966, Vol.31/498-9, p155; (20w-end) BCM, February 1966, p39; (1-19w) BCM, March 1966, p71; CHESS, April 2009, Vol.74/1, p18 (which mixes up the moves, giving 17 Rb3/19 0-0 rather than 17 0-0/19 Rb3 which is given by other sources)} (25. Bxf7 {The final point. After} Qxf7 26. Qxd6 {The threat is Qxe5+ or Qd8+, with Qf6+ in reserve if Black tries to defend with ...Qe8. Finally} h5 {, creating a square for the king on h7, loses to} 27. Qh6+ Qh7 28. Qf8+ {Looking back on the position all these years later, 1 am still amazed that I found the winning combination. (Hartston, 2009)}) 1-0

