1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 {A great favourite with Leningrad players.
The merit of this variation lies in the fact that it often leads to quite
original positions in which both players have to solve several unusual
problems.} h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 e5 ({A mistake, after which Black
has difficulty in developing his pieces harmoniously. He should have played} 
7... d6) 8. d6 $1 {This is not a new move. It was recom­mended by the
Leningrad candidate master V. G. Zak. Now White’s pawn wedge on d6 cuts off
Black’s king's side and queen’s side from each other.} g5 ({The move order
in CHESS (with Spassky's notes) and Informator is as per the text but BCM's
two sources give} 8... Nc6 9. Qc2 g5 10. Bg3 {- JS}) ({If} 8... Qa5 {White can
continue with} 9. Rc1 {with no need to fear} Qxa2 {because of} 10. Bxf6 gxf6
11. e3 {, when, in return for the sacrificed pawn, he has an excellent game.})
9. Bg3 Nc6 10. Qc2 {An improvement which helps White to gain control of the e4
and f5 squares and prevents Black’s manoeuvre 10...Ne4, followed by 11...Qf6.
} Qa5 ({My opponent embarks upon an incorrect manoeuvre. He should have
seriously thought about the development of his pieces. In my view,} 10... Nh5 {
deserved attention, aiming to continue with Qf6, b6, Bb7 and castling
queen’s side or king’s side according to events.}) 11. Rc1 {This reply,
which might at first sight seem unattractive, is, in fact, extremely effective,
since it prevents Black’s counter­play 11...Ne4. As well as this, the
threat of 12 Qf5 is now unpleasant.} Qa3 12. Nf3 {I can at last think about
the development of my king’s side.} Ne4 ({Black’s last practical chance
was in the continuation} 12... e4 13. Nd2 O-O 14. h4 Kg7 15. e3 Re8 {,
although even then Black has a very poor position.}) 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxe5
Nxd6 {And so Black has managed to eliminate the pawn on d6. He has, however,
new difficulties to cope with: his king’s side has been weakened too much.}
15. h4 gxh4 16. e3 ({If} 16. Rd1 {immediately, then} Ne4 {is possible, and
White cannot take the knight because 17...Qc3+ draws.}) 16... b6 17. Rd1 Nb7 ({
The knight must be retreated since} 17... Ke7 {is answered by} 18. Rxd6) 18.
Nxf7 {One of those sacrifices which need not be analysed thoroughly since it
is absolutely clear that Black is not in a position to defend his king.} Kxf7
19. Qf5+ Kg7 20. Qe5+ (20. Rxh4 {would also win.}) 20... Kg8 21. Rxh4 d6 22.
Qg3+ Kf8 23. Rf4+ Ke8 24. Qg7 {My prosaic 24th move destroyed my opponent’s
hopes and Lee resigned. (Sources: CHESS, January 1966, Vol.31/498-9, p158; BCM,
February 1966, p55; (1-15w) BCM, March 1966, p72; Informator 1/388)} ({After
the game, Lee said that his last hope was in the continuation} 24. Qg6+ Kd7 25.
Qg7+ Kc6 26. Qxh8 Bg4 $1 27. Qxa8 Qxc3+ {and Black wins.}) 1-0

