{"Matchett's break-through recoiled against himself." [n.b. opening moves of
this game not known, but they might have been some permutation of} 1. d4 Nf6 2.
c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. e4 g6 7. f4 Bg7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 Re8
10. O-O Na6 11. Bb5 Bd7 12. Bxd7 Nxd7 13. Nd2 Nc7 14. Qf3 b6 15. Nc4 Qf6 {
... reaching this position which is given in CHESS, with 15...Qf6 given as
Black's last move. The game continued:]} 16. e5 dxe5 17. Ne4 Qd8 18. fxe5 Nxe5
19. Nxe5 Qxd5 20. Nf6+ Bxf6 21. Qxf6 Rxe5 {"and now, against 22 Bh6, Black had
available the reply 22...Ne8. Why do we place knights on c6 when they can be
so invaluable on c7?" Source: CHESS, End-February 1966, Vol.31/501, p199;
score reconstruction, JS. No further moves and no result given with the score,
but the result given in CHESS, January 1966, Vol.31/498-9, p148, along with
the opening (Modern Benoni) and number of moves (25).} 0-1

